			<div class="content" id="post-content-2764165">
									

<p>As promised in chat.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Behold,&nbsp;the&nbsp;great&nbsp;beast&nbsp;that&nbsp;lives&nbsp;in&nbsp;the&nbsp;Wiki</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Nope</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>There’s very little time given to communication between the SCP and the Foundation, the addition of which I feel would give it more substance, and possibly answer some of the questions I have about them. Contrasted with this, you’ve got a whole paragraph about the process by which folks are turned into trees, which seems very well researched (assuming the terminology etc. is all accurate), but which doesn’t really bring anything other than some science-y stuff to the article. In my opinion you could ditch a lot of that without losing any real substance from the article.</p>
<p>On the other hand, I don’t agree with what War says about it being too fairytale-esque. I have no issue with this being based on a local fairy tale or piece of folklore, and a piece of folklore about weird forest creatures which subsequently turn out to be real works for me. YMMV of course.</p>
<p>A few thoughts about specific sections of the piece:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The known population of SCP-2831 is to be kept in a one square kilometer area surrounded by barbed wire topped chain link fence</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do we know the actual number? If so, I think it would be mentioned.<br />
Also, would chainlink be enough to keep them in? There are lots of creatures which are smart enough to climb or dig under a fence, and these seem to be sentient. I can see them scarpering pretty easily</p>
<blockquote>
<p>…and authorities in Cahir are to be notified.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, accuracy and effectiveness of containment procedures. Are we notifying the Gardai, the council, or a Foundation front hidden in the town?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They are completely built out of common ivy leaves, aside from the tips of the fingers, which appear to be sharpened sticks</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don’t like the use of ‘built’ in here, although I’ll admit I don’t have any suggestions for what to use instead.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Colonies of SCP-2831 have, as of yet, only been found in Ireland, primarily in and around the small village of ███████, near the town of Cahir. The current known population is confined to an area █.█km outside ███████ (SEE CONTAINMENT PROCEDURES).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I dislike this whole sentence. It’s long and a bit rambling, and for something with so many redactions it would be remarkably easy to locate exactly where you’re talking about, especially with local knowledge. You could probably get away with something like “The only known colonies of SCP-2831 to date have been found in the area around Cahir, Republic of Ireland” and leave it at that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When in a forested environment, specimens of SCP-2831 are usually peaceful, and generally avoid humans. However, approximately every 3 days, SCP-2831 groups will travel out of the forest to terraform new habitat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’m no David Attenborough, but this seems like an odd approach. Wouldn’t it make more sense, from a behavioural point of view, for them to gradually expand the boundaries of their territory? Then if they’re threatened they just disappear back into the forest.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Colonies of displaced specimens of SCP-2831 will seek out groups of humans (other species have been proposed to SCP-2831 colonies, all have been roundly rejected) and seek to transform them into a new home for the group.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>After a few re-reads of this sentence, I actually like it as a concept. It makes transporting them for whatever reason, or an unnoticed containment breach, a lot more hazardous. My main issue is that it seems to introduce a new behaviour, one which is more something that would happen <em>in extremis</em> than when they’re in their natural habitat.<br />
I think if they were to attack lone humans near the edge of the woods as their normal behaviour, and then have this as their emergency ‘establish a safe habitat’ mode, they would be scarier and more convincing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When the procedure is over, the human is left looking indistinguishable from a normal tree. The species of tree which the affected subject becomes varies - at the current time, no patterns have been found. Analysis of the bark of the newly created trees have revealed that only trace amounts of human biological substances are present.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don’t like the use of procedure, process fits better here in my opinion.<br />
Species of tree is important. Are the new trees at least appropriate to the environment? I’d expand on that at the expense of some of the preceding stuff about the transformation process.<br />
I like the concept of the new trees retaining some aspect of their human origins. Did you mean to just have human tissue present, or are you saying that the actual DNA is a combination of human and tree? I prefer the latter, but you’d need a real scientist to help you sort that bit out.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Another anomalous property noted by personnel is that animals seem to be attracted to the forests created by SCP-2831 colonies.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By the time you get down here, it feels like you’re throwing this paragraph in for the sake of it. I don’t object to it as a concept, but unless you go on to explain more about the process it seems tacked on. Maybe it would fit better as an addendum, combined with the information about the transformation process and the group ‘habitat building’ behaviour when removed from the forest.<br />
I could make a similar comment about the last paragraph. As the information about the hierarchy doesn’t seem to lend much to the description of what they are, it could be removed or dropped into an addendum.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I'll be honest, after we chatted the other day, I saw this coming back very different in my mind. It <em>could</em> have been something really creepy, which would have made me jumpy every time I went for a piss near a tree. As it is, I think Ramza, War and I are all saying that it's not bad, but could be a lot better.</p>

							</div>

								
					
