			<div class="content" id="post-content-2763948">
									

<p>I am inclined to upvote but will refrain - I'll list my reasons for doing so.</p>
<p>Okay, so I love this for the fact that it's almost <em>whimsical</em> with the image it builds in my mind - er, not including the whole 'turning people into trees' part.<br />
But of course this sort of whimsicality combined with a darker side does have its appeal. I mean, small humanoids turning people <em>into trees</em> definitely has a kind of creep value (I'm <em>on</em> the Irish isles gdi).</p>
<p><em>But,</em></p>
<p>I'm also inclined to say that this <em>could</em> be too fairytale-esque for a scip &#8212; I'm not necessarily saying I believe that outright, but it's something that other people may believe. Scips based off of local lore and tales, I think, would be harder to format in a way as to read like a Foundation document (though I admit that this does so quite well).</p>
<p>Also agreeing with much of what Ramza13 said - there is a lot of questions some people might ask. Are they in fact aware that the people they are transforming might not necessarily be hostile? And <em>why</em> do they <em>only</em> target people? Are animals not as efficient at producing these tree specimens, or would animals in fact be <em>immune</em> to these entities and their effects? Or is it because they seek to protect animals?</p>
<p>Neutral-vote for the time being.</p>
<p>(Also if anything I've said requires elaboration or doesn't make that much sense, let me know! I'm still learning this whole 'critting content' thing)</p>

