			<div class="content" id="post-content-3589403">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I was directed here to review from the <a href="/young-and-under-30">Young and Under 30</a> project, which is meant to get more attention to articles less than a year old, and with a score of less than +30.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this article is facing some moderate to major structural and clinical tone problems. The description just kind of meanders around, not really providing information about these creatures in an organized manner. For example, you make several statements that seem to involve the critters communicating (e.g. rejecting other animals), but you don't actually get into their linguistic ability until way further down.</p>
<p>The telling is also somewhat casual, rarely feeling like the insights shared have been substantiated or considered in any way. For example, if they go human-hunting <em>every three days</em>, why isn't that reflected in the containment procedures? Why would they specifically say &quot;governors&quot;?</p>
<p>A lot of this is made worse by a lot of the imagery being pretty ho-hum. The creature description sounds much like a D&amp;D Monster Manual entry, infection by simple touch is kinda boring, stuff testing positive for human DNA is definitely boring, and I can think of probably at least two SCPs involving turned-into-a-tree body horror. This isn't a death blow to the overall story, as I still like the idea of them going hunting and the story told in the log, but you've got to limit what isn't interesting for this to float. I'd cut the stuff about the mural and myths, for instance.</p>
<p>-1 for now.</p>

